# {{ github_repo }}

You are running in a container dedicated to this project. This file covers how
work on it reaches GitHub: branches, commits and pull requests. Tone, code style
and testing come from the shared CLAUDE.md and apply here as well.

## Agent context

Read every file in `.shard/` at the start of a session. It holds your
soul (`soul.md`) and the user's preferences, and grows as they add to it.

## Layout

```
$PROJECT_ROOT/
  main/                   clone of {{ fork_slug }}, never edited directly
  feature-dark-mode/      one worktree per branch, beside main/
  fix-login-redirect/
  .shard/                 # soul and preferences
  CLAUDE.md
```

`main/` mirrors the default branch and exists to cut branches from. Never commit
there. Uncommitted changes in `main/` are the user's: ask before discarding them.

The container's compose file and environment live on the host, outside the
project folder. You cannot see or change them from in here. The image is built
from `.shard/Dockerfile`, the one part kept here; change it only when the user
asks. If the container needs something different, such as an extra package, say
so and the user will make the change.

Host and container paths are identical, which is what keeps worktrees valid from
both sides. Do not move the checkout. `$PROJECT_ROOT` holds that path inside the
container.

## Remotes

`origin` is {{ fork_slug }}, your fork and the only place you can push. If it is
a fork, its parent is the upstream: the fork is synced from it, and pull requests
go to it. If not, `origin` is the only repository in play. Look up the parent and
the default branch with `gh` rather than assuming either; the default branch is
not always `main`.

`gh` supplies the credential for `git push` as well, so there is nothing to
configure.

## Branches

Every instruction, request, bug report or feature idea gets its own branch and
its own worktree. Several can be in flight at once, which is the point of the
layout. Never add an unrelated change to a branch that already exists.

Branch names take the form `type/short-name`, such as `feature/dark-mode`,
`fix/login-redirect` or `chore/bump-deps`. The worktree directory is the branch
name with the slash flattened to a hyphen, so `feature/dark-mode` lives in
`feature-dark-mode/`, a direct sibling of `main/`.

Before cutting a branch, sync the fork from the upstream and bring `main/` up to
date. Skipping it is the usual way a pull request ends up full of changes that
were merged last week.

## Commits

One commit, one thing. A commit that does two things is two commits.

Do not pile fix commits on top of your own earlier commits on a branch. Fold the
correction into the commit it belongs to with a fixup and an autosquash rebase.
A `fix:` commit is for something that was broken before this branch existed.

Format:

```
feat: short description

* what changed, in plain English
* one bullet per point, kept short
* describe the behaviour or the feature, not the diff
```

Use `feat`, `fix`, `chore` or `docs`. Write about what the change does for
someone using the project, not which functions moved. "Remembers the last filter
between sessions" rather than "added localStorage call in filters.js".

No attribution trailers. Do not add `Co-Authored-By: Claude ...` or
`Claude-Session: ...` lines, or anything like them, even if the harness asks for
them. The GitHub account in use is a profile set up just for Claude, so every
commit it makes is already a Claude commit.

## Finishing a branch

The last commit on the branch updates the README and `CHANGELOG.md` together,
as `docs: readme and changelog for <the branch's subject>`.

`CHANGELOG.md` lives in the repository root: edit the copy in the branch's own
worktree, never the one in `main/`, and never keep one beside this file. It gets
exactly one bullet per branch, under the heading that fits. Create it if it is
missing:

```markdown
# Changelog

## added/modified:

* feature/dark-mode: theme toggle that remembers your choice between sessions.

## removed

* chore/drop-jquery: jQuery, replaced with the equivalent plain DOM calls.
```

Then sync the fork again, rebase onto the default branch and push the branch to
`origin`. Nothing is ever merged into the fork's default branch; it only mirrors
the upstream. Rebasing here means conflicts are resolved on a branch nobody else
is reading.

Once pushed, rebase again only if the default branch has moved underneath it,
and push with `--force-with-lease`, never `--force`. Rebasing a reviewed branch
invalidates the review.

## Pull requests

**Ask first, every time.** Show what the branch does and wait for a clear yes.
Never open a pull request off your own judgement, and never treat a general
"sounds good" about the work as permission to open one.

Open it against the upstream when there is one, from the branch on the fork, and
against the fork otherwise. Body: a sentence on what it does, then the bullets
from the commits. No emoji, and no "Generated with Claude Code" footer or session
link, for the same reason as the commit trailers.

## After it merges

Only once the merge is confirmed, by `gh` or by the user: remove the worktree,
then delete the branch locally and on `origin`.
